From: NanoSoC het-chiplet testing
Subject: [PATCH] fix(fpga): correct the compute die_b role strap — H6

`kr260-compute-chiplet` carries die_b's BALL MAP with die_a's STRAP, so
pairing it with the eth die_a image gives TWO MASTERS and no slave.

The conductors are already right and must not be touched:
    eth  kr260-eth-chiplet      pad_clk_tx = AD15   (strap 0, master)
    cmp  kr260-compute-chiplet  pad_clk_tx = AC14   <- the eth die's RX ball
so this image IS the correct partner for eth die_a. Only the role is wrong.

Do NOT "fix" this by switching to -flip. That image has pad_clk_tx = AD15 —
the same ball the eth die transmits on — so both dies would drive every
ribbon conductor. KR260_BENCH_RUNBOOK.md already forbids it, and two 3.3 V
CMOS drivers at DRIVE 8 fighting is a stress condition, not just a dead link.

NOTE the naming consequence: after this, kr260-compute-chiplet (non-flip) is
the die_b image, which INVERTS the eth convention where non-flip = die_a.
Worth renaming the targets to -die-a/-die-b, or at minimum saying so in
BUILD_NOTES.md — otherwise the next person reads the name and deploys the
wrong pair.

Verify with no board: L0-BUILD-04 in the het test repo is xfail(strict) and
will FAIL once this lands. L0-BUILD-05 (conductors complementary) passes both
before and after — that is the check that stops a "fix" via the ball map.

diff --git a/tidelink/fpga/targets/kr260-compute-chiplet/tidelink_design.tcl b/tidelink/fpga/targets/kr260-compute-chiplet/tidelink_design.tcl
--- a/tidelink/fpga/targets/kr260-compute-chiplet/tidelink_design.tcl
+++ b/tidelink/fpga/targets/kr260-compute-chiplet/tidelink_design.tcl
@@ -159 +159 @@
-    set_property -dict [list CONFIG.CONST_WIDTH {1} CONFIG.CONST_VAL {0}] $strap_const
+    set_property -dict [list CONFIG.CONST_WIDTH {1} CONFIG.CONST_VAL {1}] $strap_const
